# Phase 8 — std.javascript corpus (complete: all 116 cases handled)
# 115 valid cases parse cleanly; the 1 deliberately-malformed case
# (destructuring__5) is asserted to be rejected, matching tree-sitter flagging
# it as an error (tree-sitter recovers into an (ERROR)/(MISSING) tree, tinyparse
# rejects — both detect it). Full error-recovery-tree parity is future work.

# Phase 8 — std.typescript / std.tsx corpus (complete: all 112 cases parse)
# Every vendored tree-sitter TypeScript corpus case is valid TypeScript (none of
# the expected trees carries an (ERROR)/(MISSING) node), so every tsfamily test
# is an accept test, run against std.typescript or — for the :language(tsx) cases
# — std.tsx, and all parse cleanly.
#
# Eight of those accept tests also carry structural assertions (see
# suite/tsfamily/gen-corpus-suite.py): an accept-only check passed even when the
# grammar built a degenerate tree, so the cases whose tree turns on a parser
# decision (function signature vs declaration, an asserts/predicate return type,
# an import(...) type) now assert the deciding node counts and would catch a
# regression to a clean-but-wrong parse.
#
# Two cases (types__39, functions__10) place a comment under a different parent
# than tree-sitter does. The comment is present as a node either way; only its
# attachment differs, which is a structural limit of the extras model rather than
# a grammar bug. docs/comment-attachment.md proposes the fix.
